 ### [IsosurfaceClassifier Lighting Model](6676-isosurfaceclassifier-lighting-model.md)
-The IsosurfaceClassifier Lighting Model is a surface-shading technique for thresholded volumes that visualizes an isosurface by treating volume samples as surface points with opacity determined by a Gaussian transfer function, computing color using a Phong illumination model (ambient, diffuse, specular) with normals derived from the volume gradient. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Interval Shading](6414-interval-shading.md) by applying explicit surface lighting calculations (Blinn-Phong) rather than interval-based color mapping, and from [Volume Raycasting with Surface Extraction and Phong Shading](3662-volume-raycasting-with-surface-extraction-and-phong-shading.md) by integrating the lighting directly into the classifier's transfer function evaluation during ray marching.
+The IsosurfaceClassifier Lighting Model is a volume rendering technique that visualizes an isosurface within a volume dataset by treating samples as surface points with opacity determined by a Gaussian transfer function, and computing color using a Phong illumination model (ambient, diffuse, specular) with normals derived from the volume gradient. It applies surface-like shading to volumetric data near an isovalue, with transparency based on distance from the isovalue.
 
